WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.018
There were days when a reaction had more observers than

00:00:04.018 --> 00:00:07.944
glassware. In Chem Squad, a bench was also a place to

00:00:07.944 --> 00:00:09.728
argue through the chemistry together.

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:20.076
It was easy to obtain an extract and much harder to say

00:00:20.076 --> 00:00:22.774
what it contained. Identifying a constituent would

00:00:22.774 --> 00:00:25.858
still be different from proving a useful biological

00:00:25.858 --> 00:00:26.244
effect.

00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:34.822
The group that became Chem Squad included seven

00:00:34.822 --> 00:00:37.772
chemistry graduates. There was no grand institution

00:00:37.772 --> 00:00:41.565
behind us. We kept bringing papers, arguments, and new

00:00:41.565 --> 00:00:43.672
questions back to the conversation.

00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:50.888
Building the ACS student chapter demanded work

00:00:50.888 --> 00:00:54.817
invisible in a spectrum: persuading people to join,

00:00:54.817 --> 00:00:57.763
finding instruments and keeping questions moving.

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:03.659
We organized demonstrations and scientific discussions.

00:01:03.659 --> 00:01:06.869
Through chapter activities, twenty-nine students had

00:01:06.869 --> 00:01:11.148
opportunities to train on instruments. The work was

00:01:11.148 --> 00:01:14.357
collective; the photographs deserve collective credit.

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:18.316
Chemistry festivals taught me another form of

00:01:18.316 --> 00:01:21.591
precision: speaking clearly when the audience has every

00:01:21.591 --> 00:01:25.684
reason to question you, and resisting the urge to make

00:01:25.684 --> 00:01:27.322
a demonstration seem conclusive.

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:34.854
As years passed, we moved into different specialties.

00:01:34.854 --> 00:01:37.407
Some conversations continued through weekly meetings,

00:01:37.407 --> 00:01:41.236
and some through disagreements sent across a phone long

00:01:41.236 --> 00:01:43.363
after a class had ended.

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:50.662
What survived was not one experiment or a photograph

00:01:50.662 --> 00:01:54.230
with familiar faces. It was the habit of calling each

00:01:54.230 --> 00:01:56.014
other back to the chemistry.
